\description{
Fits the same model at latent rank (number of ordination axes) \verb{d = 1, ..., d_max} by sweeping the \code{d} argument of the formula's single ordinary
\code{latent(...)} covstruct term, and reports AIC, BIC, and AICc for each. The
fit at the \code{criterion}-minimising rank is reported as \code{selected_d}.

\code{select_lv()} is a \strong{information-criterion workflow tool}, not a
hypothesis test: it says nothing about statistical significance and
carries no interval on the chosen \code{d}. For a likelihood-ratio test of one
additional latent dimension (with the appropriate boundary correction, and
an explicit refusal when that correction is not justified), see
\code{\link[=anova.gllvmTMB_multi]{anova.gllvmTMB_multi()}}.
}
\section{Scope}{
Every fit in the sweep uses ordinary maximum likelihood (\code{REML = TRUE} is
rejected — AIC/BIC comparisons across different random-effect structures
under REML are not meaningful when the induced conditioning changes with
\code{d}, and gllvmTMB's REML route is Gaussian-only regardless). A single
failing \code{k} (an error from \code{\link[=gllvmTMB]{gllvmTMB()}}, non-convergence, or a non-PD
Hessian) does not abort the sweep; it is recorded in the table with \code{NA}
criteria and excluded from selection, with a warning naming which \code{d}
failed and why.
}

\section{Guard against a non-nesting or runaway fit}{
A rank-\code{d} model nests the rank-\code{(d - 1)} model (a zero loading column), so
a correctly maximised fit can never have a lower log-likelihood than the
best of every smaller rank already tried. A fit is excluded from selection
-- its row stays in \code{table} with \code{status} naming why, but it is never
chosen -- when: it errors (\code{"failed"}); the optimizer did not converge or
the Hessian is confirmed non-positive-definite (\code{"unconverged"}); its
loadings are runaway (\code{"runaway"}, see \code{max_latent_sd}/\code{ratio_max} above);
or its log-likelihood falls below \verb{max(tol, 1e-6 * |bar|)} of \code{bar}, the
best log-likelihood among every converged, non-runaway fit at any smaller
\code{d} (accepted or itself rejected as non-monotone; a runaway fit's inflated
log-likelihood never becomes \code{bar}) (\code{"nonmonotone"}). By default
(\code{require_converged = FALSE}), a fit whose optimizer did not report
convergence is \strong{kept} rather than excluded on that flag alone -- it is
still subject to the runaway and monotonicity checks above, and only
rejected (\code{"unconverged"}) when its Hessian is CONFIRMED non-positive-
definite. Under the loading ridge that Hessian is the penalised one (the
objective the optimiser minimised), not \code{sdreport()}'s unpenalised one,
which can be indefinite at a proper penalised optimum. On the auto-d
recovery grid (13,506 simulated datasets), rejecting on the convergence
flag alone lowered Poisson recovery (0.999 to 0.991); re-measured on the
corrected NB fitting code (4,794 datasets), it lowered negative-binomial
recovery from 0.934 to 0.747, because a dispersion at the Poisson
boundary is reported as unconverged. Every broken
unconverged fit in that grid was already caught by the runaway or
monotonicity checks; \code{require_converged = TRUE} restores the stricter
rule. With \code{warm_start = TRUE} (the default), a rejected fit is retried once with \verb{control(start_from = <the last accepted fit>)} before being excluded, and kept (\code{status = "warm_start"}) if the retry passes every check. This is a retry from the
last accepted fit's matching parameter blocks only, NOT a retry "from the
(d - 1) solution": \code{gllvmTMB}'s \code{start_from} route copies same-shaped
blocks (fixed effects, dispersion) but never the loading matrix, whose
shape differs between ranks, so the new loading column in the retry still
starts at the ordinary default init. Healthy sweeps never trigger a retry,
so their results are unchanged from a version of this function without the
guard.
}

\section{Binary (single-trial Bernoulli) loading ridge and the monotonicity bar}{
For single-trial binomial data (see \code{binary_ridge}), every fit is
penalised: the optimiser minimises \code{likelihood_nll + 0.5 * sum(lambda^2) / tau^2}, not the plain likelihood. Nesting then only guarantees that this
PENALISED objective improves with \code{d} -- the unpenalised log-likelihood at
the ridge's MAP point can fall even for a correctly maximised larger
model. So whenever the ridge is active (from \code{binary_ridge} or from the
caller's own \code{control(aghq_ridge = )}), the non-monotone guard and \code{bar}
above compare the penalised objective (\code{-(likelihood_nll + penalty)},
read off the fit's own \code{objective_components}), not the log-likelihood.
\code{criterion} selection is unaffected: \code{bic_sites}/\code{bic}/\code{aic}/\code{aicc} always
use the unpenalised log-likelihood at that MAP point, exactly as
\code{logLik()} reports it (with its usual warning that this is a MAP-point
likelihood, muffled per-fit and replaced by one summary message for the
whole sweep). Without the ridge (the default for any other family, or for
multi-trial binomial data), this section does not change anything.

The rank \code{d} chosen by \code{select_lv()} is itself an estimate: standard
errors, confidence intervals, and tests computed on \code{selected_fit}
(\code{sel$fits[[as.character(sel$selected_d)]]}) are conditional on that \code{d}
and do not include the uncertainty of having selected it.
}

\examples{
\donttest{
set.seed(7)
n_units <- 70L
traits <- paste0("t", 1:4)
units <- paste0("u", seq_len(n_units))
Lambda_true <- matrix(
  c(0.8, 0.5, -0.6, 0.4, 0.3, 0.7, -0.5, 0.6),
  nrow = 4, ncol = 2
)
scores <- matrix(rnorm(n_units * 2), n_units, 2)
eta <- tcrossprod(scores, Lambda_true)
dat <- do.call(rbind, lapply(seq_len(n_units), function(i) {
  data.frame(unit = units[i], trait = traits,
    value = eta[i, ] + rnorm(4, sd = 0.2))
}))
dat$unit <- factor(dat$unit, levels = units)
dat$trait <- factor(dat$trait, levels = traits)

sel <- select_lv(
  value ~ 0 + trait + latent(0 + trait | unit, d = 1),
  data = dat, unit = "unit", trait = "trait", d_max = 3,
  criterion = "bic",
  control = gllvmTMBcontrol(optimizer = "optim", optArgs = list(method = "BFGS"))
)
sel
sel$selected_d
}

}
